#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/correct {:answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554310} :answer-field/key "selection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554487}] :question/math-academy-id "q-138860" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}}{\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6/3}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3x+6)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x(3x+6)-({x}^{2}+2)}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{11{x}^{2}+24x-2}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/correct {:answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554239} :answer-field/key "selection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554464}] :question/math-academy-id "q-340513" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]}
